[Page:Bugs]
SectionPrefix=Bug

[Bug:jump:JUMP ONTO the barrel jumps into a message]
The barrel's own JUMP ONTO handler, #R$AA27, refuses a jump from anywhere but directly above it. Where a way to the barrel exists but is not down, it does <code>JP NZ,$B301</code> -- and $B301 is the message "you cannot jump onto the ... from here", not code. It was surely meant to be <code>LD HL,$B301</code> and <code>JP</code> RUN_MESSAGE, as the line before it does. A quick test from the great halls beside the cellar did not reach it, because the parser will not name a barrel in another room; whether anything can reach it is not worked out.

[Bug:fill:FILL looks as though it can never work]
The barrel's FILL WITH, #R$8F69, fetches a fresh water and puts it in the barrel with the objects swapped, through PUT IN (#R$924F). But PUT IN begins with the last part of CAN_LIFT, which refuses a liquid -- and the water is a liquid. As read, then, filling the barrel is always refused. This has not been confirmed in play.

[Bug:unreached:Code nothing reaches]
Seven stretches of the game read as code but nothing calls them, jumps to them or names them in a table the game uses: special word slot 0's handler with the code before it (#R$82FD), an OPEN for the wood elf alone (#R$A536), a routine that would wipe an exit (#R$A70A), one that finds what an object holds (#R$A1AE), and three fragments (#R$78FB, #R$9030, #R$92E8). They are left as data here, with what they would do said beside them.

[Page:Credits]
SectionPrefix=Credit

[Credit:prior:Prior work]
This disassembly is re-derived from the v1.2 tape, but others went before it, and their work was consulted for where to look -- credited, not copied:
<ul>
<li><a href="https://github.com/pobtastic/hobbit">pobtastic's SkoolKit disassembly</a> of the same tape (<a href="https://pobtastic.github.io/hobbit/">browse it</a>), which named the save, load and printing routines first.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.icemark.com/dataformats/hobbit/">Icemark's notes on the data formats</a>.</li>
<li>A complete annotated SkoolKit disassembly of the v1.0 tape by an author it does not name. v1.0's addresses are not v1.2's, so it was used only to know what exists.</li>
</ul>

[Credit:tools:Tools]
Built with <a href="https://skoolkit.ca">SkoolKit</a>: its simulator played the game to find the code, drew every location's picture for the locations page, and printed every message the pages quote.
